---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Start from '@/components/motion/scenes/Start.astro';
import { StartControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/StartControls';
import { MotionPresenceReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-presence.tsx';
import { MotionPresenceAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-presence.ts';
import MotionPresenceSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-presence.svelte';
import WeightTable from '@/components/motion/WeightTable.astro';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="getting-started"
  title="Motion: getting started"
  description="Install @udixio/motion, connect it once, and attach a named motion to an element in HTML, React, Angular or Svelte."
>
  <MotionStage scene="start" label="Live example: Getting started" height={272}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><StartControls client:visible /></Fragment>
    <Start />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class="block">npm i @udixio/motion animejs</span><span class="block">connectMotion()</span><span class="block">data-motion="slide"</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <p>
    <code>@udixio/motion</code> plays motions on the elements of a page. A motion is defined once, by name, and attached to an
    element by that name: with <code>data-motion</code> in HTML, or through the adapter of your framework. The runtime is
    framework-agnostic; its only animation engine is <a href="https://animejs.com">anime.js</a>, which you install alongside it.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <h3>Install</h3>
  <pre><code>npm install @udixio/motion animejs</code></pre>
  <p>The stylesheet of the tokens is optional. Import it in the document stylesheet to expose the spring and duration variables and <code>--udx-motion-scale</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>@import '@udixio/motion/motion.css';</code></pre>

  <h3>Connect once</h3>
  <p>Start the runtime once per page, with the presets you use. <code>connectMotion()</code> binds the <code>data-motion</code> elements under the document and keeps them in step with the DOM. It returns a function that disconnects them.</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { connectMotion } from '@udixio/motion';
import '@udixio/motion/presets';

const disconnect = connectMotion();`}</code></pre>
  <p>An element that is on the page when the runtime connects plays no entry. An element inserted later plays its entry, and an element that is hidden plays its exit.</p>

  <h3>Define and attach</h3>
  <p>Define a motion with <code>defineMotion</code>, then name it in the markup. The presets are motions that are already defined: see <a href="/motion/presets">Presets and verbs</a>.</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

defineMotion('card-enter', {
  verb: 'appear',
  keyframes: [
    { opacity: 0, y: 24 },
    { opacity: 1, y: 0 },
  ],
  timing: 'spring.default.spatial',
});`}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{`<div data-motion="card-enter">…</div>
<div data-motion="fade">Fades in and out</div>`}</code></pre>

  <h3>Without a bundler</h3>
  <p>
    The built files run in a page with no bundler: serve <code>dist/</code> with the page, and map the entries you import in
    an import map. The other files of <code>dist/</code> are imported relative to their own URL, so they need no mapping.
    A page has no <code>process</code>, so its development checks are off, as in a production build;
    <code>connectMotion(&#123; development: true &#125;)</code> still reports the runtime messages.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`<script type="importmap">
{
  "imports": {
    "@udixio/motion": "/vendor/motion/dist/index.js",
    "@udixio/motion/presets": "/vendor/motion/dist/presets.js"
  }
}
</script>
<script type="module">
  import { connectMotion } from '@udixio/motion';
  import '@udixio/motion/presets';

  connectMotion();
</script>`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The live examples render a card with the <code>slide</code> preset, and each framework keeps it until its exit has played.
    The adapters hold no animation: the motion is still defined and timed by <code>@udixio/motion</code>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>React: <code>&lt;Motion name="slide"&gt;</code> from <code>@udixio/ui-react</code>, in a <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> so that a removed card plays its exit.</li>
    <li>Angular: add <code>Motion</code> to the component's <code>imports</code>, then <code>&lt;div udxMotion="slide"&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li>Svelte: <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('slide')&#125; transition:presence</code> on an element of an <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> block.</li>
  </ul>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-presence" previewHeight={[248, 256]}>
    <MotionPresenceReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionPresenceAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionPresenceSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>
  <p>Every need of the section is shown live on its <a href="/motion">Animation overview</a>, with its code.</p>

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>The slide preset moves on the default spatial spring and fades on the default effects spring. A motion of your own names its timing; see <a href="/motion/tokens">Springs and tokens</a>.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>Under reduced motion (<code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>, or <code>data-motion-policy="reduce"</code> on <code>html</code>), entries and exits become a short fade, and an emphasis applies its end frame. See <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="weight">Weight and loading</h2>
  <p>
    The core binds every motion, and it is loaded with the page. Each other entry loads the first time a page needs it, or when
    you import it. Each weight is the budget the package's build enforces, gzipped: an entry is at most that size.
  </p>
  <WeightTable />

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The package is under construction; see <a href="/motion/limits">Limits</a> for the current list.</li>
    <li>A layout motion loads its engine the first time it is bound; a change made before that is not animated. <code>loadLayoutEngine()</code> loads it in advance.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
